  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nVisible satellite at 11 AM was showing marine layer low clouds \nslowly clearing from the coast. The inversion on the 12Z KNKX \nsounding was about 10 degrees so clearing at the beaches this \nafternoon may be patchy. Low clouds will remain constant at the \ncoast each night and morning through the forecast period, reaching\ninto portions of the western valleys at times. \n\nThursday through Saturday highs will be above average near the coast \nto 5 to locally 10 degrees above average for some inland areas. Low \ntemperatures will also run above average, most noticeable in the \nmountains and deserts where lows will be 10 to 15 degrees above \naverage. For the Inland Empire and mountains, widespread moderate \nHeatRisk is expected which means that folks who are sensitive heat, \nespecially those without consistent cooling/hydration will be most \nimpacted. In the deserts, major to locally extreme HeatRisk \nindicates anyone may see negative impacts from the heat if \nprecautions aren't taken. Make sure to stay hydrated and if possible \navoid strenuous activity during the hottest parts of the day (10 AM \nto 4 PM). \n\nThe flow aloft will turn southeasterly Thursday and remain \nsoutheasterly into early next week. This would allow for some \nmonsoonal moisture into the area and bring a 15 to 20 percent chance \nof showers and isolated thunderstorms to the mountains each \nafternoon. Through Friday, there is only a 15 percent chance of \nrainfall rates exceeding 0.50\" per hour in any showers that develop \nin the mountains. Current forecast follows the NBM which has chances \neach afternoon, with most widespread coverage Sunday and Monday.",
